%S A399635 6,10,19,21,26,33,55,56,66,68,76,87,91,96,115,125,140,150,164,208,209,
%T A399635 222,230,285,286,290,306,308,336,399,406,425,440,441,468,475,476,490,
%U A399635 546,595,616,637,664,696,700,705,710,728,731,738,770,780,805,825,854,868
%N A399635 Positive integers k such that R^2*cos(A)*cos(B)*cos(C) = 36*k for a primitive acute integer-sided triangle with circumradius R and angles A, B, C.
%C A399635 Hippocrates' theorem states that the two lunes constructed on the legs of a right triangle have a combined area equal to the triangle area. For an arbitrary triangle, construct an outward semicircle on each side and subtract the corresponding circumcircle segment outside that side. If S is the sum of these three signed differences and T the triangle area, then S - T = Pi*q, where q = R^2*cos(A)*cos(B)*cos(C). Thus S = T for right triangles, S > T for acute triangles, and S < T for obtuse triangles.
%C A399635 For primitive integer-sided triangles (gcd(x, y, z) = 1), q is an integer if and only if the circumdiameter 2*R is an integer, in which case 36 divides q. The sequence lists the distinct positive values k = q/36.
%C A399635 Only the residue classes 0, 3, 5, and 6 (mod 7) occur, and each contains infinitely many terms. The set of terms has natural density zero.
%H A399635 Felix Huber, <a href="/A399635/b399635.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1350</a>.
%H A399635 Felix Huber, <a href="/A399635/a399635.txt">Maple program</a>.
%H A399635 Felix Huber, <a href="/A399635/a399635.pdf">Proofs for the positive integral lune-excess spectrum</a>.
%H A399635 Martin Lukarevski, <a href="https://doi.org/10.4171/EM/317">An alternate proof of Gerretsen's inequalities</a>, Elemente der Mathematik, 72 (2017), 2-8.
%H A399635 Ronald van Luijk, <a href="https://doi.org/10.4171/EM/96">The diameter of the circumcircle of a Heron triangle</a>, Elemente der Mathematik, 63 (2008), 118-121.
%H A399635 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/HeronianTriangle.html">Heronian Triangle</a>.
%H A399635 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Lune.html">Lune</a>.
%F A399635 a(n) mod 7 is in {0, 3, 5, 6}.
%F A399635 For every epsilon > 0, a(n) = Omega_epsilon(n^(5/4 - epsilon)).
%F A399635 a(n) = O(n^3).
%F A399635 In particular, a(n)/n -> infinity as n -> infinity.
%e A399635 a(1) = 6 because the primitive acute triangle (68, 75, 77) has circumdiameter d = 85 and q = (68^2 + 75^2 + 77^2 - 2*85^2)/8 = 216 = 36*6.
%e A399635 a(2) = 10 because the primitive acute triangle (105, 116, 143) has circumdiameter d = 145 and q = 360 = 36*10.
%e A399635 a(25) = 286 has three representations, from the triangles (425, 1073, 1092), (427, 1500, 1517), and (525, 548, 667), each with q = 10296 = 36*286.
